WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.548
Resistance tells how strongly a component opposes the flow of current.

2
00:00:05.548 --> 00:00:11.096
That does not mean current stops completely.

3
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:16.644
It means a larger resistance makes it harder for charge to flow for a given potential difference.

4
00:00:16.644 --> 00:00:22.192
This lesson becomes clearer when resistance is treated as a relationship between voltage and current rather than as a mysterious extra object in the circuit.

5
00:00:22.192 --> 00:00:27.740
Once that relationship is stable, Ohm's law and the I-V graph start to feel like two views of the same idea.

6
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.288
Keep this takeaway in view: Resistance is about how strongly a component opposes current flow.

7
00:00:33.288 --> 00:00:38.836
Resistance means opposition.

8
00:00:38.836 --> 00:00:44.384
A larger resistance makes charge flow harder for the same voltage.

9
00:00:44.384 --> 00:00:49.932
Voltage means push.

10
00:00:49.932 --> 00:00:55.480
Potential difference drives the charges through the component.

11
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.673
The I-V graph is powerful because it shows the relationship directly.

12
00:01:01.673 --> 00:01:07.867
When the current is proportional to the voltage, the graph is a straight line through the origin.

13
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:14.060
That means doubling the voltage doubles the current, tripling the voltage triples the current, and the ratio stays stable.

14
00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:20.253
This is the classic ohmic resistor pattern.

15
00:01:20.253 --> 00:01:26.447
The graph is not only a picture of two variables.

16
00:01:26.447 --> 00:01:32.640
It is a visual check that the component keeps a constant resistance under those conditions.

17
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.833
Keep this takeaway in view: For an ohmic resistor, the I-V graph proves the proportional relationship.

18
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:45.027
Straight line: The current changes in direct proportion to the voltage.

19
00:01:45.027 --> 00:01:51.220
Through origin: Zero voltage gives zero current for the resistor.

20
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:56.094
Imagine people trying to move through a corridor.

21
00:01:56.094 --> 00:02:00.968
If the corridor is clear, people can move through more easily.

22
00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:05.842
If the corridor is narrow and crowded, movement becomes harder.

23
00:02:05.842 --> 00:02:10.717
That is similar to resistance in a circuit.

24
00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:15.591
The battery provides the push, but the component determines how difficult the path is for charge flow.

25
00:02:15.591 --> 00:02:20.465
A larger resistance is like a harder corridor for the moving charges.

26
00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:25.339
The analogy is helpful because it links the word opposition to something physical and intuitive.

27
00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:30.213
Keep this takeaway in view: Resistance is the difficulty of the path for charge flow.

28
00:02:30.213 --> 00:02:35.088
Easy path means low resistance.

29
00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:39.962
Charges can flow more readily for the same voltage.

30
00:02:39.962 --> 00:02:44.836
Difficult path means high resistance.

31
00:02:44.836 --> 00:02:49.710
Charges face more opposition, so the current is smaller.

32
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:54.584
The formal relation is R equals V divided by I.

33
00:02:54.584 --> 00:02:59.458
Read it as resistance equals potential difference divided by current.

34
00:02:59.458 --> 00:03:04.332
For a given resistor under steady conditions, that ratio stays constant, which is why the graph stays straight for an ohmic resistor.

35
00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:09.207
The formula does not replace the meaning.

36
00:03:09.207 --> 00:03:14.081
It summarizes it.

37
00:03:14.081 --> 00:03:18.955
If the same resistor needs more voltage to produce a certain current, then its resistance is larger.

38
00:03:18.955 --> 00:03:23.829
If a resistor allows more current for the same voltage, then its resistance is smaller.

39
00:03:23.829 --> 00:03:28.703
Keep this takeaway in view: R = V / I captures how voltage and current relate through a component.

40
00:03:28.703 --> 00:03:33.578
Large R: For the same voltage, the current is smaller.

41
00:03:33.578 --> 00:03:38.452
Small R: For the same voltage, the current is larger.

42
00:03:38.452 --> 00:03:43.326
I = V / R.

43
00:03:43.326 --> 00:03:48.200
Use the voltage across the component and the current through it.

44
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:55.136
When current is directly proportional to voltage, each increase in voltage produces a matching increase in current.

45
00:03:55.136 --> 00:04:02.071
That is why the graph is straight and why the ratio V divided by I stays constant.

46
00:04:02.071 --> 00:04:09.007
This pattern is worth securing because it lets learners move between the table, the graph, and the formula without treating them as separate topics.

47
00:04:09.007 --> 00:04:15.943
They are three descriptions of the same ohmic behavior.

48
00:04:15.943 --> 00:04:22.879
Keep this takeaway in view: Table, graph, and formula are all telling the same resistance story.

49
00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:29.814
Table view: If voltage doubles and current doubles, the relationship is proportional.

50
00:04:29.814 --> 00:04:36.750
Graph view: A straight line through the origin shows the same proportional pattern.

51
00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:42.975
A common mistake is to see a larger voltage in one situation and assume the resistance must also be larger.

52
00:04:42.975 --> 00:04:49.200
That does not follow automatically.

53
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.425
If the current also rises in proportion, the resistance may stay exactly the same.

54
00:04:55.425 --> 00:05:01.650
Resistance is the ratio between voltage and current for the component, not just the size of one number by itself.

55
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:07.875
This is why the graph pattern is so useful: it keeps the relationship visible instead of encouraging isolated number thinking.

56
00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:14.100
Keep this takeaway in view: Judge resistance from the relationship, not from one number on its own.

57
00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:20.325
Resistance is not just the voltage value.

58
00:05:20.325 --> 00:05:26.550
Resistance depends on the relationship between voltage and current.

59
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:30.261
Ask three quick questions.

60
00:05:30.261 --> 00:05:33.972
What does resistance describe?

61
00:05:33.972 --> 00:05:37.682
Opposition to current flow.

62
00:05:37.682 --> 00:05:41.393
What graph pattern shows an ohmic resistor?

63
00:05:41.393 --> 00:05:45.104
A straight line through the origin on an I-V graph.

64
00:05:45.104 --> 00:05:48.815
And what formula links resistance, voltage, and current?

65
00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:52.525
R equals V divided by I.

66
00:05:52.525 --> 00:05:56.236
Those are the three anchors this lesson wants you to keep stable before solving numbers.

67
00:05:56.236 --> 00:05:59.947
Keep this takeaway in view: If the relationship is clear, the resistance calculation is only the final step.

68
00:05:59.947 --> 00:06:03.658
Meaning means opposition.

69
00:06:03.658 --> 00:06:07.368
Resistance tells how hard the component makes the flow.

70
00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:11.079
Graph clue means straight through origin.

71
00:06:11.079 --> 00:06:14.790
That pattern shows current proportional to voltage.

72
00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:21.114
You are ready for the worked example now.

73
00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:27.438
If the resistor has 12 volts across it and a current of 3 amperes through it, use R equals V divided by I.

74
00:06:27.438 --> 00:06:33.762
Twelve divided by three gives 4 ohms.

75
00:06:33.762 --> 00:06:40.086
Keep the meaning attached: this resistor needs 12 volts to drive 3 amperes, so its opposition to current is 4 ohms.

76
00:06:40.086 --> 00:06:46.410
On the graph side, the straight line through the origin confirms the current is proportional to the voltage for this ohmic resistor.

77
00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:52.734
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: relationship first, numbers second, meaning still attached.

78
00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:59.058
Step 1: Read the voltage across the component and the current through it.

79
00:06:59.058 --> 00:07:05.382
Step 2: Use R = V / I to calculate the resistance.

80
00:07:05.382 --> 00:07:11.706
R = 12 / 3.

81
00:07:11.706 --> 00:07:18.030
Connect the calculation to the straight-line I-V graph pattern.
